<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Maximilien Robespierre</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1758">1758</a>–<a href="/years/1794">1794</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Maximilien Robespierre was a French lawyer and leading Jacobin of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolution">French Revolution</a>. Elected to the Estates-General in 1789 and to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-convention">National Convention</a> in 1792, he joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/committee-of-public-safety">Committee of Public Safety</a> in July 1793 and became the most prominent figure of the Terror, which he defended as necessary to save the Republic. He was overthrown by the Convention on 9 Thermidor (27 July 1794) and guillotined the next day.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/maximilien-robespierre.jpg" alt="Maximilien Robespierre" width="320" height="396" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unidentified painter · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Robespierre.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Oliver Cromwell’s Protectorate of 1653–1658 was described as a military dictatorship by his enemies and by some modern scholars, and <b>Maximilien Robespierre</b> was called a dictator while the Committee of Public Safety directed the Reign of Terror in France in 1793–1794.</blockquote><a href="/government/dictatorship">Dictatorship · Through History</a></li><li><blockquote>Facing war and revolt, the Convention gave power to the Committee of Public Safety under <b>Robespierre</b>; the Terror executed thousands before his fall in July 1794.</blockquote><a href="/state/french-first-republic">French First Republic</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arras">Arras</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=50.29,2.78&amp;year=1781" aria-label="Arras on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4614 416 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Maximilien Robespierre"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5064 686h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5064 686h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=50.29,2.78&amp;year=1781">What was here in 1781? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;We wish in our country that morality may be substituted for egotism, probity for false honour, principles for usages, duties for good manners, the empire of reason for the tyranny of fashion…&#8221;</p><footer>Report on the Principles of Political Morality, February 1794 &#183; anonymous translation, Philadelphia 1794 &#183; <a href="https://sourcebooks.fordham.edu/mod/1794robespierre.asp" rel="noopener">Internet History Sourcebooks, Fordham University</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;We wish, in a word, to fulfill the intentions of nature and the destiny of man, realize the promises of philosophy, and acquit providence of a long reign of crime and tyranny.&#8221;</p><footer>Report on the Principles of Political Morality, February 1794 &#183; anonymous translation, Philadelphia 1794 &#183; <a href="https://sourcebooks.fordham.edu/mod/1794robespierre.asp" rel="noopener">Internet History Sourcebooks, Fordham University</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;When however, the government alone want it, there exists a resource in that of the people; but when the people themselves are corrupted liberty is already lost.&#8221;</p><footer>Report on the Principles of Political Morality, February 1794 &#183; anonymous translation, Philadelphia 1794 &#183; <a href="https://sourcebooks.fordham.edu/mod/1794robespierre.asp" rel="noopener">Internet History Sourcebooks, Fordham University</a></footer></blockquote><details class="enc-q-more"><summary>3 more</summary><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;If virtue be the spring of a popular government in times of peace, the spring of that government during a revolution is virtue combined with terror: virtue, without which terror is destructive; terror, without which virtue is impotent.&#8221;</p><footer>Report on the Principles of Political Morality, February 1794 &#183; anonymous translation, Philadelphia 1794 &#183; <a href="https://sourcebooks.fordham.edu/mod/1794robespierre.asp" rel="noopener">Internet History Sourcebooks, Fordham University</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;The government in a revolution is the despotism of liberty against tyranny.&#8221;</p><footer>Report on the Principles of Political Morality, February 1794 &#183; anonymous translation, Philadelphia 1794 &#183; <a href="https://sourcebooks.fordham.edu/mod/1794robespierre.asp" rel="noopener">Internet History Sourcebooks, Fordham University</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;It is with regret that I utter this fatal truth. But Louis must die, because the country must live.&#8221;</p><footer>Speech to the National Convention against a trial for Louis XVI, 3 December 1792 &#183; tr. in The World's Famous Orations, ed. William Jennings Bryan, 1906 (translator not named) &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_World%27s_Famous_Orations/Volume_7/Against_Granting_the_King_a_Trial" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote></details><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 535 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
